//! {{project-name}}: gameplay shared between `cargo run` and the
//! editor's Play button.
//!
//! Scene content (entities, components, positions) lives in
//! `assets/*.bsn` files authored in the editor. Game behaviour
//! (systems, observers, resources) lives in [`GamePlugin`].
//!
//! # Try it
//!
//! 1. Open this project in jackdaw.
//! 2. Outliner: click `Add Entity` and pick `Cube`.
//! 3. Components panel: click `Add Component` and pick `SpinningCube`.
//!    Set `speed` to `1.5`.
//! 4. `File > Save`, then click Play: the menu comes up first — click
//!    **Start** and the cube spins in the level.
//! 5. `cargo run` launches the same game without the editor.
//!
//! # Adding your own components
//!
//! Write a component anywhere in this library (any module, not
//! `main.rs`), deriving `Component, Reflect, Default` with
//! `#[reflect(Component, Default)]`, like `SpinningCube` below. After you
//! save, click Rebuild in jackdaw (or run `jd build`) and it
//! appears in `Add Component`. No registration code is needed.
//!
//! # Tuning parameters (game-wide, saved as an asset)
//!
//! `GameConfig` below is the other half of the pattern: a *Resource*
//! whole systems read (`falling_cubes` uses `gravity` and
//! `move_speed`) whose value comes from an asset file,
//! `assets/game-config.bsn`. To change the tuning:
//!
//! 1. In jackdaw, `Project window > New Asset...` and pick
//!    `GameConfig` (after one Rebuild) — the editor creates
//!    `game-config.bsn` and opens its card.
//! 2. Edit `move_speed` (say `2.5`), `Save`.
//! 3. Play: the falling cube falls and spins by the file's numbers.
//!    `cargo run` and an exported build read the same file.
//!
//! Until that file exists the game runs on the `Default` values — a
//! missing config is not an error. Per-entity numbers (one cube's
//! `speed`) stay as components; game-wide numbers (gravity, move
//! speed, player health) belong in a config like this.
//!
//! # Input actions
//!
//! The same pattern drives input: `InputActionsPlugin` evaluates the
//! built-in actions (Move / Look / Jump / Interact / Pause — WASD and
//! the left stick, mouse and the right stick, Space / South, E / East,
//! Escape / Start) into the `GameActions` resource every frame; systems
//! read named actions instead of polling raw keys. To rebind, author
//! `assets/input-actions.bsn` the same way as the tuning config (the
//! plugin below wires the file; without it, the built-in table runs).
//!
//! In the editor's Play, click the game image to capture input and
//! Shift+Esc to give it back — a plain Esc is forwarded to the game
//! (that is why Pause can be Escape).
//!
//! # Physics & triggers
//!
//! The physics module (on by default) adds avian: things fall, land
//! and stay on colliders. Three pieces worth knowing:
//!
//! * **Colliders**: add an `AvianCollider` component to any entity
//!   (the editor's physics category) and pick a shape. A collider
//!   with no rigid body stands still as a wall or floor; a
//!   `RigidBody: Dynamic` one falls and lands — never through a
//!   floor. The falling cube and the pickups below are the examples.
//! * **Triggers**: an entity that is a collider *and* carries
//!   `Sensor` detects overlaps without blocking anything (the editor
//!   draws sensors with a cyan outline). Each frame,
//!   `TriggerEvents` lists every sensor that started or stopped
//!   touching something: `enters()` for pickup-style "got it",
//!   `exits()` for "left the area". The plugin wires the
//!   `CollisionEventsEnabled` prerequisite avian asks for — author
//!   the `Sensor`, nothing else. `collect_pickups` below is the
//!   whole pattern: the player walks into a pickup, it despawns and
//!   the count goes up — only the player's touch counts, so a cube
//!   settling onto a pickup leaves it alone; collect all three and
//!   the level clears.
//! * **Collision layers**: a `CollisionLayers` component (fields
//!   `memberships` and `filters`, one bit per layer) decides who
//!   interacts with whom — a contact only exists when each side's
//!   memberships meet a layer the other side filters in. A common
//!   table: layer 1 the player and the world, layer 2 pickups,
//!   layer 3 hazards. Give hazards `memberships 0b100,
//!   filters 0b001` (hazard hits player only) and the player
//!   `0b001, 0b111` (hits everything) — pickups and hazards never
//!   interact with each other, so a drifting hazard cannot collect
//!   the pickups for the player. `spawn_hazards` below authors
//!   exactly that hazard row; the template player needs nothing
//!   (an entity without the component is on layer 1 with every
//!   filter, which is the table's player row already).
//!
//! The triggers feed more than pickups: `collect_pickups` counts and
//! `hazard_damage` burns health — same shape, different marker and
//! different reaction, and both read the one `TriggerEvents` the
//! bridge fills.
//!
//! Two gravities exist and they are different things:
//! `GameConfig.gravity` (the config asset above) is the author's
//! number, and at startup `apply_gravity_from_config` copies it
//! into the physics engine's `Gravity` resource — the one the
//! simulation actually uses. Editing the config file changes the
//! next launch; changing the `Gravity` resource in the editor's
//! Resources panel changes it for the running session.
//!
//! # Player controller
//!
//! The player the template spawns at (-2, 1, 0) is a real, if
//! minimal, character controller:
//!
//! * **Tuning is a component.** `PlayerController { move_speed,
//!   jump_speed }` lives on the player entity: select it and edit
//!   the numbers in the Components panel (per-entity numbers are
//!   components; game-wide numbers are the config above — the same
//!   split as one cube's `speed` vs the config's `move_speed`).
//! * **Movement is camera-relative.** W walks away from the camera
//!   wherever it looks; the body turns smoothly to face the way it
//!   moves. The one source of truth for "which way is forward" is
//!   the camera's `CameraLook.yaw`.
//! * **The camera is a child.** A `CameraRig` parented under the
//!   player is the whole follow-camera setup: `JackdawCameraRigPlugin`
//!   (wired in `GamePlugin` below) activates a lone rig by itself and
//!   drives it around its parent every frame. Author a second rig
//!   only together with an `ActiveCameraRig` component — with two
//!   rigs none self-activates; the game must choose.
//! * **Look has two channels that agree.** The `Look` action writes
//!   the rig's `CameraLook` every frame (`look_into_camera` below);
//!   while the right mouse button is held the rig's own mouse-look
//!   owns the turn instead — the bridge yields those frames so the
//!   two never double-write.
//! * **Jump and collision come from the physics module** (on by
//!   default): gravity — the very same `GameConfig.gravity` number
//!   the engine got — integrates onto the player's velocity, a short
//!   downward cast says when the feet are on the ground, and avian's
//!   `move_and_slide` sweeps the capsule so the player slides along
//!   walls instead of through them (walk into the stage walls to see
//!   it: head-on you stop, diagonally you slide). Without the physics
//!   module the player still walks, camera-relative and
//!   frame-rate-independent, but does not jump or collide.
{{module_physics_player_doc}}
//!
//! # Game flow
//!
//! A game is more than one world: a menu to start from, the level to
//! play, an end screen when the run is over. The template ships all
//! three as real scene documents — `assets/menu.bsn`, `assets/scene.bsn`,
//! `assets/gameover.bsn` — and only one of them is in the world at a
//! time:
//!
//! * **States drive the worlds.** `GameFlow` below is a `States` enum
//!   (`Menu` / `Level` / `GameOver`). Each state's `OnEnter` shows its
//!   document by re-binding one scene root's handle — the runtime's
//!   whole switch primitive is that one insert, and the old scene's
//!   entities (its UI roots included) are gone before the new document
//!   spawns. Nothing accumulates across a menu → level → game over →
//!   menu loop.
//! * **Where a run begins is data.** `assets/game.flow.json` names the
//!   scene a standalone run starts from (`start_scene`) and lists the
//!   scenes that make up the game. The game boots into whatever it
//!   names: the menu document as the menu, the game-over document as
//!   the end screen, any other scene as a world to play. A broken or
//!   mis-pointed file warns and falls back — it never refuses to start.
//!   The editor validates the file, the Project window's right-click
//!   **Set as Startup Scene** writes it, and an export ships it.
//! * **What survives a switch is decided on purpose.** The player and
//!   everything your code spawns (health, counters, resources) belong
//!   to no scene document, so a switch leaves them alone — the player's
//!   entity id is one and the same from Start to Restart. Scene content
//!   (pickups, hazards, the hud) belongs to its world and dies with it.
//!   What crosses gets put back in shape instead: `reset_run` runs on
//!   every entry into the level, so Restart is just a switch to `Level`.
//! * **Each world brings its own camera.** The level's camera is the
//!   rig under the player; the menu and game-over documents get a
//!   state-scoped `Camera2d` (`DespawnOnExit` removes it with the
//!   state) — a UI document shown alone has nothing to render it
//!   otherwise, and the screen stays empty.
//! * **The editor's two Play buttons stay distinct.** Play runs the
//!   game from its flow-configured start scene; the scene-tab Play
//!   swaps the editor's own scene in as the world the game boots into,
//!   and the flow state follows it (that boot is the level).
//!
//! # Game UI
//!
//! `assets/hud.bsn` is a UI scene: the same kind of document as
//! `scene.bsn`, authored on the editor's 2D canvas (`Add` menu, `UI`
//! group — panels, labels, buttons, progress bars) with each widget's
//! `Bindings` component wiring it to the game. It is the level's own
//! overlay — the template spawns it when the level is current and takes
//! it away when the level ends — and its two screens show and hide
//! themselves:
//!
//! * **Screens follow state.** The pause screen's `Visibility` is bound
//!   to `GameState.paused` (the resource below). Flipping the bool is
//!   the whole mechanism — overlays inside one world are data, not
//!   switches; the worlds themselves are the flow above.
//! * **Buttons say which one was pressed.** Every button carries an
//!   `Action` binding that sends `ScreenPressed` with `button` set to
//!   its literal (`"resume"` here; the menu and game-over documents
//!   send `"start"`, `"restart"` and `"menu"`); the
//!   `on_screen_pressed` observer turns those into flow moves. The
//!   Pause *action* (Escape) lands on the same bool through
//!   `handle_pause_action`, so the key and the button agree.
//! * **The bar and the texts read the player.** The health bar's
//!   `Progress.value` is bound to `PlayerHealth.current` and `.max`
//!   through the registered `ratio` function, and the texts are
//!   `Text` bindings on the same components. Bindings can only call
//!   functions the game registers — that is what the
//!   `register_function` lines below are for. `wire_hud` is the
//!   other half: it puts a `BindContext(player)` on the document's
//!   `HudRoot` once it spawns, which is what `PlayerHealth...`
//!   resolves against.
//!
//! Edit `hud.bsn` in the editor, `Save`, and the running game picks
//! it up like `scene.bsn`. Layout discipline for your own screens:
//! percent and flex for anything position-like (the pause overlay
//! stretches to 100% of the window, the HUD hugs the top-left corner),
//! pixels only for sizes and padding — that is what keeps the screens
//! correct from 800x600 up to 2560x1440. One editor edge to know:
//! Play with a UI scene tab focused swaps the 3D scene for that UI
//! alone (it previews the canvas); play from the 3D scene tab to run
//! the game.


use bevy::prelude::*;
use jackdaw_runtime::prelude::*;

/// Your game's Bevy plugin. `main.rs` adds this for both `cargo run`
/// and the editor's Play button (same binary).
#[derive(Default)]
pub struct GamePlugin;

impl Plugin for GamePlugin {
    fn build(&self, app: &mut App) {
        // The boot scene, read once: the editor's Play override, then
        // the project's `game.flow.json`, then the template's menu. The
        // same string maps to the initial `GameFlow` state below and is
        // loaded by `spawn_scene`, so the state machine and the first
        // document can never disagree.
        let start = jackdaw_runtime::pie_scene_or(&jackdaw_runtime::flow::start_scene_or(
            MENU_SCENE,
        ));
        app.insert_resource(BootScene(start.clone()))
            .init_state::<GameFlow>()
            // Inserting the boot state runs its `OnEnter` on the first
            // frame — the game is born in the right world, not switched
            // into it later.
            .insert_state(boot_state(&start))
            // `spawn_hud` in the Update watcher, never before the main
            // root: the editor's Play swaps whichever scene root landed
            // at the LOWEST entity index, and a hud root below the game
            // root means Play swaps the hud document instead of the
            // world (T12-T2 caught this live; the flow re-spawns the hud
            // per level entry, so the ordering is pinned here every
            // time, not just at startup).
            .add_systems(Startup, spawn_scene)
            .add_systems(Update, spin_cubes)
            // One line per config type: register it as an asset (so the
            // editor offers it in New Asset) and load `game-config.bsn`
            // as the starting value of the `GameConfig` resource.
            .init_asset::<GameConfig>()
            .register_asset_reflect::<GameConfig>()
            .add_plugins(ConfigPlugin::<GameConfig>::default())
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::Level), spawn_falling_cube)
            .add_systems(Update, falling_cubes)
            // Input: the built-in five actions evaluate every frame; the
            // `input-actions.bsn` asset (same New Asset flow) overrides
            // bindings and deadzones when the game registers the type.
            .init_asset::<GameInputConfig>()
            .register_asset_reflect::<GameInputConfig>()
            .add_plugins(InputActionsPlugin)
            .add_plugins(ConfigPlugin::<GameInputConfig>::new(
                "input-actions.bsn",
            ))
            // The player spawns once — on the first entry into the level
            // — and then crosses every switch untouched (see
            // `spawn_player`; `reset_run` puts its numbers back).
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::Level), (spawn_player, reset_run).chain())
            .add_systems(Update, (move_player, look_into_camera))
            // The camera rig driver: activates the rig under the player
            // and follows it every frame (see "Player controller" above).
            .add_plugins(JackdawCameraRigPlugin)
            // Game flow (see "Game flow" above): the per-state document
            // swaps and cameras, the level-scoped hud, the observer the
            // flow documents' buttons feed, and the systems that move
            // the state. Types (`GameFlow`, `GameState`, `PlayerHealth`,
            // `ScreenPressed`) need no registration — the `Reflect`
            // derives announce them the same way they reach the editor's
            // Add Component list; the via functions are the one binding
            // piece a game registers by hand.
            .init_resource::<GameState>()
            .register_function(ratio)
            .register_function(is_zero)
            .register_function(not)
            .add_observer(on_screen_pressed)
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::Menu), enter_menu)
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::Level), enter_level)
            .add_systems(OnEnter(GameFlow::GameOver), enter_game_over)
            .add_systems(Update, spawn_hud_while_level)
            .add_systems(
                Update,
                // Chained: the player spawns with a zeroed `PlayerHealth`,
                // and an `update_game_over` that ran first would read
                // current <= 0 on frame one and boot the game straight
                // into game over (T12-T2 caught this live in PIE).
                (wire_hud, (apply_health_from_config, update_game_over).chain()),
            )
            // The pause flip owns the frame: a Pause press never also
            // moves the player, whichever way the bool lands. Registered
            // HERE and only here — adding it twice (tuple + edge) runs
            // the toggle twice per press and Escape never pauses
            // (T12-T2 caught this live).
            .add_systems(Update, handle_pause_action.before(move_player));
{{module_physics_statements}}
    }
}

/// Spawn the one scene root the flow re-binds: `JackdawSceneRoot` is
/// the runtime's switch primitive, and re-inserting it with a new
/// handle is a whole world swap (the old document's entities despawn,
/// the new one spawns when it loads). The handle is the boot scene —
/// `game.flow.json`'s `start_scene` unless the editor's Play put one of
/// its own there — the same string `boot_state` mapped the initial
/// state from. One root for all three worlds: the flow moves the
/// document under it, and the root itself (and this marker) is the
/// stable thing across every switch.
fn spawn_scene(
    mut commands: Commands,
    boot: Res<BootScene>,
    asset_server: Res<AssetServer>,
) {
    commands.spawn((
        Name::new("GameRoot"),
        GameRoot,
        JackdawSceneRoot(asset_server.load(boot.0.clone())),
    ));
}

/// Spin-rate in radians per second. Attach it in the Components panel; the
/// editor sees this component with no registration code because Bevy's
/// `reflect_auto_register` picks up the `Reflect` derive. `Default` +
/// `#[reflect(Default)]` let the editor add it and the game reconstruct
/// it with sensible starting values.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct SpinningCube {
    pub speed: f32,
}

fn spin_cubes(time: Res<Time>, mut cubes: Query<(&SpinningCube, &mut Transform)>) {
    let dt = time.delta_secs();
    for (cube, mut transform) in &mut cubes {
        transform.rotate_y(cube.speed * dt);
    }
}

/// Game-wide tuning parameters. Both an `Asset` (the editor authors
/// `game-config.bsn` files of it) and a `Resource` (systems read it),
/// so the value can be edited in the editor, saved to disk, and loaded
/// back as the starting value in every run — see "Tuning parameters"
/// above. `player_health` is the run's starting health:
/// `apply_health_from_config` hands it to the player's `PlayerHealth`
/// component when the file loads — the config is the author's intent,
/// the component is the live state after that.
#[derive(Asset, Resource, Reflect, Clone)]
#[reflect(Resource, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Game Config"))]
pub struct GameConfig {
    /// The health a run starts at, handed to the player's
    /// `PlayerHealth` (see "Game UI" above).
    pub player_health: f32,
    /// Multiplies how fast things move and spin.
    pub move_speed: f32,
    /// Downward acceleration, in units per second squared.
    pub gravity: f32,
}

impl Default for GameConfig {
    /// Sensible numbers a game runs on before anyone has authored
    /// `game-config.bsn` — a missing config is not an error.
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            player_health: 100.0,
            move_speed: 1.0,
            gravity: -9.81,
        }
    }
}

/// A cube the config drives, spawned so there is something to watch:
/// it falls under gravity and spins at its component's `speed`
/// scaled by `GameConfig::move_speed` — the per-entity number and
/// the game-wide number working together.
///
/// With the physics module (the default) the fall is the engine's:
/// the cube is a dynamic body with its own collider, dropped onto
/// the stage `spawn_stage` lays below it, so it lands and stays.
/// The spin stays the game's own — a system, not a force.
#[derive(Component, Reflect, Default)]
#[reflect(Component, Default, @EditorCategory::new("Actor"))]
pub struct FallingCube {
    pub speed: f32,
}

fn spawn_falling_cube(
    mut commands: Commands,
    mut meshes: ResMut<Assets<Mesh>>,
    mut materials: ResMut<Assets<StandardMaterial>>,
) {
    // No `SpinningCube` here: two systems rotating one entity stack
    // their rates, which reads as the config having no effect. The
    // falling cube's spin belongs to `move_speed` alone, so one value
    // changes one behavior.
    // The cube is level scenery: spawned with the level, gone with it
    // (`DespawnOnExit`), back fresh the next time the level starts.
    commands.spawn((
        FallingCube { speed: 1.0 },
        Mesh3d(meshes.add(Cuboid::new(0.6, 0.6, 0.6))),
        MeshMaterial3d(materials.add(StandardMaterial {
            base_color: Color::srgb(0.9, 0.9, 0.9),
            ..default()
        })),
        Transform::from_xyz(0.0, 3.0, 0.0),
        DespawnOnExit(GameFlow::Level),
{{module_physics_falling_body}}
    ));
}

fn falling_cubes(
    time: Res<Time>,
    config: Res<GameConfig>,
    mut cubes: Query<(&FallingCube, &mut Transform)>,
) {
    let dt = time.delta_secs();
    for (cube, mut transform) in &mut cubes {
